QB.add(61,[{"n":1,"q":"A 25-year-old woman presents with fatigue, joint pain, and a photosensitive rash. She reports mouth ulcers and hair loss. Laboratory tests show positive ANA and elevated anti-ds DNA antibodies. Further examination reveals elevated levels of IFN-a. Which of the following is a consequence of elevated IFN-a in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Decreased B cell activation"},{"t":"Enhanced clearance of apoptotic cells"},{"t":"Suppression of T cell activity"},{"t":"Increased antibodies production"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of fatigue, joint pain, photosensitive rash, mouth ulcers, hair loss, positive ANA and anti-dsDNA antibodies suggest systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated levels of IFN-a play a crucial role in SLE pathogenesis by promoting B cell activation and increasing antibody production , particularly autoantibodies such as anti-dsDNA. Predisposing/Risk factors of SLE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic factors Environmental factors"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complement deficiencies of C1q, C2, and C4 Gender: Female TREX1 gene mutations OCPs and HRT Affecting antigen presentation of HLA-DRB, DR3, DQA2 Ultraviolet light Enhanced innate immunity , mainly increased Smoking production of IFNs Silicosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Vitamin D deficiency EBV infection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis of SLE: Decreased B cell activation (Option A): IFN-a promotes B cell activation and survival, not decreases it . This contributes to autoantibody production in SLE."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Enhanced clearance of apoptotic c ells (Option B): In SLE, clearance is defective , contributing to accumulation of nuclear debris fi triggers autoimmunity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Suppression of T cell activity (Option C): IFN-a activates dendritic cells and promotes T cell activation , not suppression."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a history of recurrent fever, myalgia, photosensitivity, and joint pain. On examination, multiple erythematous plaques with adherent scales are noted on her face and scalp. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Urticarial eruptions"},{"t":"Malar rash"},{"t":"Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus (SCLE)"},{"t":"Discoid lupus erythematosus"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms of recurrent fever, myalgia, photosensitivity, and joint pain, along with erythematous plaques with adherent scales on the face and scalp, suggest Discoid Lupus Erythematosus (DLE), a chronic cutaneous form of lupus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterised by round, scaly, slightly raised, erythematous plaques with hyperpigmented rims and depigmented centres that occur due to damage to all the dermal appendages."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical manifestations of SLE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Constitutional Fever, fatigue, myalgia, weight loss symptoms"},{"k":"img","v":8701},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mucocutaneous symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Musculoskeletal Joint pain with synovitis involving more than two joints, symptoms typically non-erosive but occasionally leading to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"deformities in severe cases."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anemia Due to autoimmune reactions, it can happen without"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"other organ involvement."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neuropsychiatric Cognitive impairment, mood disorders, psychosis, involvement stroke, seizures, peripheral neuropathy, etc."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lung and Renal This can be seen in 50% of patients affected with SLE. involvement Pulmonary manifestations may include pleuritis,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary hypertension. Renal involvement typically presents as lupus nephritis, characterised by proteinuria, hematuria, and, in severe cases, progressive renal dysfunction."}],"qi":[8682]},{"n":3,"q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with chest pain worsened by deep breathing, shortness of breath, fatigue, joint pain, and a photosensitive facial rash. Physical examination reveals tenderness over the chest wall and mild joint swelling. Laboratory tests show positive ANA and elevated anti-dsDNA antibodies. A chest X-ray shows mild pleural effusion. Which of the following pulmonary complications is most likely associated with her condition? (ARDS: Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome)","o":[{"t":"ARDS"},{"t":"Shrinking lung syndrome"},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension"},{"t":"Pleuritis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation of chest pain that worsens with deep breathing, along with a history of fatigue, joint pain, photosensitivity, and positive autoimmune markers (ANA, anti-dsDNA), suggests Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleuritis is the most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE, and it can present with or without effusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can be managed by NSAIDs in its mild form and with a short course of glucocorticoids in case of severe pleuritis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Systemic Involvement in SLE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"System Manifestations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pulmonary system Pleuritis (most common): Prevalence (30 - 50%)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pneumonitis Atelectasis Reduced lung volume and Pulmonary nbrosis Shrinking lung syndrome, (< 5%) (Option B ruled out) Intra-alveolar hemorrhage Pulmonary HTN (< 5 %) (Option C ruled out) Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) (< 5%) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8707},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Musculoskeletal system"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Renal system Lupus nephritis (most common)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Proteinuria Nephrotic syndrome Rarely End-stage Renal Disease (ESRD)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nervous system Headache, Seizures, Stroke, Polyneuropathy, TIA,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Acute confusional state, Movement disorder, Aseptic meningitis, Myelopathy, Psychosis and Mood disorders."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiovascular Coronary artery disease - common cause of death system Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Coronary vasculitis Myocarditis Libman sacks endocarditis Accelerated atherosclerosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hematological Anemia of chronic disease manifestations Leucopenia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lymphopenia Thrombocytopenia Autoimmune Hemolytic anemia-MCC of anemia in SLE Splenomegaly"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"GI involvement Abdominal pain"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Nausea, diarrhoea Abnormal liver enzymes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ocular manifestation Sicca syndrome: Dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and dry mouth (xerostomia). Conjunctivitis Episcleritis Vasculitis"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with joint pain and skin rashes after being treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a recent infection. She has a history of Systemic lupus erythematosus. Laboratory tests reveal positive anti-dsDNA antibodies. Which of the following antibodies would be more specific for diagnosing the cause of her symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Anti-Ro"},{"t":"Anti-RNP"},{"t":"Anti-C1q"},{"t":"Antihistone"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antihistone antibodies are the most specific antibodies for diagnosing drug-induced lupus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common drugs associated with drug-induced SLE are (Mnemonic: SHIP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S ulfa drugs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"H ydralazine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"I soniazid"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P rocainamide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dapsone Autoantibodies of SLE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antibodies Antigen recognised Clinical significance"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ANA Multiple nuclear antigens Best screening test for diagnosis, it"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"is highly sensitive but not very specific for SLE."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-dsDNA DNA (double-stranded) High titers are specific for SLE"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Used to correlate with disease severity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-C1q Collagen-like determinants on Associated with active lupus (Option C ruled C1q nephritis , it is non-specific for SLE out) and can be used to correlate with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"disease activity. Deficiency predisposes SLE."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-Sm Protein complexed to 6 species It is highly specific for SLE."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"of nuclear U1 RNA."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-RNP (Option Protein complexed to U1 RNA Non-specific to SLE and is B ruled out) associated with syndromes that"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"overlap with rheumatic syndromes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-Ro (SS-A) Protein complexed to hY RNA Not very specific , it is associated (Option A ruled 9 (primarily 60 kDa and 52 kDa) with sicca syndrome and neonatal out) lupus (congenital heart block)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti-La (SS-B) 47-kDa protein complexed to Commonly associated with Anti-Ro"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hY RNA antibody"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antihistone Histones associated with DNA Associated with drug-induced lupus. Antiphospholipid Phospholipids, b2-glycoprotein Usually predisposes to clotting, fetal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"1 cofactor, prothrombin loss and thrombocytopenia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti Erythrocyte Erythrocyte membrane Causes autoimmune hemolytic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"anemia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antiplatelet Surface and altered It contributes to thrombocytopenia,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cytoplasmic antigens on is not very specific or sensitive, and platelets is not used in regular practice."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antineuronal Neuronal and lymphocyte Occasionally, it shows a positive antibody surface antigens correlation with active CNS lupus."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti Ribosomal P Protein in ribosomes Associated with neuropsychiatric"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"manifestations."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 27-year-old woman presents with joint pain, fatigue, and a malar rash. Laboratory tests reveal positive ANA and anti-dsDNA antibodies. According to the EULAR/ACR classification criteria, which of the following criteria is not required for diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Antiphospholipid antibodies"},{"t":"Serositis"},{"t":"Neuropsychiatric symptoms"},{"t":"Ocular symptoms"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Though GI and ocular symptoms are part of SLE manifestations, they aren't included in the EULAR/ACR Classification Criteria for SLE."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This classification includes both clinical & immunological criteria; the patient must fulfil at least one clinical criterion, and ‡10 points are classified."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ANA positive ( titer of ‡ 1:80 on Hep-2 cells) is the required entry criteria to classify SLE."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"EULAR/ACR Classification Criteria for SLE"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Clinical Domain and We Immunological Domain We Criteria igh and Criteria igh"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"t t"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Constitutional 2 Antiphospholipid 2 Fever antibodies (Option A ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out) Anti-cardiolipin antibodies or Anti-b2GP1 antibodies or Lupus anticoagulant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hematologic 3 Complement proteins 3 Leukopenia 4 Low C3 or low C4 4 Thrombocytopenia 4 Low C3 and C4 Autoimmune hemolytic anemia Neuropsychiatric (Option 2 SLE – specinc antibodies 6 C ruled out) 3 Anti-ds DNA antibody or Delirium 5 Anti-Smith antibody Psychosis Seizure"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mucocutaneous 2 Non-scarring alopecia 2 Oral ulcers 4 Subacute cutaneous OR 6 discoid lupus Acute cutaneous lupus"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Serosal (Option B ruled 5 out) 6 Pleural or pericardial en usion Acute pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Musculoskeletal 6 Joint Involvement"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Renal 4 Proteinuria >0.5 g/24 h 8 Renal biopsy Class II or V 10 lupus nephritis Renal biopsy Class III or IV lupus nephritis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"A positive ANA test is required as an entry criterion. A diagnosis is made if a total score of ‡10 points is reached based on clinical and immunological features. All the criteria should be mainly explained by SLE but not be attributed to other medical conditionsor factors."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 25-year-old woman presents with fatigue, a malar rash, and joint pain. Laboratory tests show positive anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith antibodies, and a positive ANA. She undergoes a renal biopsy that reveals features consistent with lupus nephritis. Based on the SLICC criteria, which of the following statements is true?","o":[{"t":"Four clinical criteria must be met to confirm SLE"},{"t":"Renal biopsy indicating lupus with lupus autoantibodies is sufficient"},{"t":"At least 2 clinical criteria with one antibody confirms SLE"},{"t":"Presence of Anti-ds, Anti-Sm and ANA confirms SLE"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The SLICC (Systemic Lupus International Collaborating Clinics) criteria state that SLE can be diagnosed if a patient meets 4 criteria, including at least 1 clinical and 1 immunological criterion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, SLE can also be diagnosed if a renal biopsy shows lupus nephritis and the patient has positive ANA or anti-dsDNA antibodies, regardless of meeting other clinical or immunological criteria. Systemic Lupus International Collaborating Clinic (SLICC) Criteria"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Manifestations Immunological Manifestations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Skin: ANA > reference negative Acute cutaneous lupus erythematosus (photosensitive, value malar, maculopapular, bullous) Anti-dsDNA > reference Chronic cutaneous lupus erythematosus (discoid lupus, (if by ELISA, 2× reference) panniculitis, lichen planus-like, hypertrophic verrucous, Anti-Sm chill blains) Antiphospholipid antibodies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lupus anticoagulant Anticardiolipin Anti–b2-glycoprotein1 Low serum complement (C3, C4, or CH50) Positive direct Coombs test"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Oral or nasal ulcers"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nonscarring alopecia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Synovitis involving ‡2 joints"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serositis (Pleurisy or pericarditis)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Renal: Protein-to-creatinine ratio (Prot/Cr) ‡0.5 RBC casts Renal biopsy indicating lupus (Wire-loop lesions)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurologic: Seizures Psychosis Mononeuritis & myelitis Peripheral/Cranial neuropathies Acute confusional state"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hematology: Hemolytic anemia Leukopenia (<4000/mL) Lymphopenia (<1000/mL) Thrombocytopenia (<100,000/mL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification Criteria:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At least 4 criteria must be present , with at least 1 from the clinical manifestations and 1 from the immunologic manifestations. (Option A, C and D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A renal biopsy indicating lupus nephritis qualifies as systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) if any lupus autoantibodies are present, even if the total criteria are < 4. (Option B)"}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 32-year-old woman with a known history of systemic lupus erythematosus presents with hypertension and elevated creatinine levels. A renal biopsy reveals glomerular inflammation affecting over 50% of her glomeruli. Based on these findings, which class of lupus nephritis does she fall under?","o":[{"t":"Class V"},{"t":"Class II"},{"t":"Class III"},{"t":"Class IV"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's renal biopsy shows diffuse glomerular inflammation involving over 50% of glomeruli, and immunofluorescence shows wire-loop lesions corresponding to Class IV lupus nephritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification of Lupus nephritis"},{"k":"img","v":8715},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class I: Minimal Mesangial Lupus Nephritis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class II: Mesangial Proliferative Lupus Nephritis Light microscopy : Mesangial hypercellularity (or) matrix (Option B ruled out) expansion"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mesangial immune deposits Subepithelial or subendothelial deposits may be present. Lupus Nephritis \" data-author=\"Elshweikh, Samah & Elkhouly, Radwa & Shaalan, Mohamed & Shorbagy, Safinaz\" data-hash=\"9918\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Class- II-lupus-nephritis-showing-proliferation-of-mesangial-cell s-arrow-H-E-X400_fig1_274257010\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/nlMnET1tF0fHvgdu62MP1744449341.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lupus Nephritis\" data-author=\"Habib, Rumana & Islam, Reezwan & Miah, Md & Islam, Md Rashedul & Rahman, Aminur & Bhowmik, Nirmalendu.\" data-hash=\"9923\" data-license=\"CC BY NC 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www. researchgate.net/figure/Fig-6-Mesangial-proliferative-lup us-nephritis-Class-II-lupus-nephritis_fig5_320308764\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"307\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"AGbU77uC1744449436.png\" width=\"364\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class III: Focal Lupus Nephritis Active or inactive glomerulonephritis involving £50% of Class III (A): Active lesions—focal proliferative all glomeruli. lupus nephritis. Focal subendothelial immune deposits and possible Class III (C): Chronic inactive lesions—focal mesangial alterations sclerosing lupus nephritis. Focal Lupus Nephritis\" data-author=\"Anders, Class III (A/C): Active and chronic lesions Hans-Joachim & Fogo, Agnes.\" data-hash=\"9928\" (Option C ruled out) data-license=\"\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.n"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"et/figure/Focal-lupus-nephritis-ISN-RPS-class-III-a-There -is-endocapillary-proliferation-and-a_fig2_259630750\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"273\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"NGsLjOStp1744449627.png\" width=\"346\" />"},{"k":"img","v":8718},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class IV: Diffuse Lupus Nephritis Class IV-S: Diffuse segmental lupus nephritis (‡50% of involved glomeruli have segmental lesions). Class IV-G: Diffuse global lupus nephritis (‡50% of involved glomeruli have global lesions). Class IV (A): Active lesion Class IV (A/C): Active and chronic lesion Class IV (C): Chronic inactive lesions with scars (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Active or inactive glomerulonephritis involving ‡50% of all glomeruli. Diffuse subendothelial immune deposits and possible mesangial alterations. Wire-loop lesions in IF ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":8721},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class V: Membranous Lupus Nephritis (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Global or segmental subepithelial immune deposits. Morphologic sequelae are observable by light microscopy, immunofluorescence, or electron microscopy, with or without mesangial alterations. ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Class VI: Advanced Sclerotic Lupus Nephritis ‡90% of glomeruli globally sclerosed."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"No residual activity. Sclerotic Lupus Nephritis\" data-author=\"Giannico, Giovanna & Fogo, Agnes.\" data-hash=\"9945\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.n et/figure/Global-sclerosis-in-lupus-nephritis-Despite-the-e xtensive-degree-of-sclerosis-the_fig2_230850562\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/uCmY10nv6PvfQY5Lshrh1744450426.png\" />"}],"qi":[8690]},{"n":8,"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a history of SLE presents with increased fatigue, severe joint pain and pleuritis with features suggestive of coronary artery disease. Laboratory tests indicate elevated serum creatinine. Which of the following management strategies is most appropriate for this patient?","o":[{"t":"High-dose glucocorticoids and Cyclophosphamide"},{"t":"Hydroxychloroquine and NSAIDs for symptom relief"},{"t":"Low-dose glucocorticoids, along with azathioprine"},{"t":"Conservative management with close monitoring and supportive care"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"For SLE patients with severe disease presenting with significant organ involvement, the most appropriate management strategy is high-dose glucocorticoids and cyclophosphamide ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physical and lifestyle modifications like stress reduction techniques, smoking cessation, photoprotection and diet rich in vitamin D."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Analgesics and NSAIDs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydroxychloroquine (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucocorticoids (Option C) (prednisolone 5–20 mg/day) - often combined with immunosuppressants such as: Methotrexate Azathioprine Mycophenolate mofetil (MMF)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Methotrexate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycophenolate mofetil (MMF)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased glucocorticoid doses may be needed for flares (e.g., pleurisy, pericarditis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Belimumab (a monoclonal antibody targeting BLyS) and anifrolumab are effective for persistent disease activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Methotrexate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycophenolate mofetil (MMF)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-dose glucocorticoids and immunosuppressants for organ involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common Regimen: Pulsed methylprednisolone IV (10 mg/kg) for 3 days, followed by oral steroid. Cyclophosphamide (15 mg/kg IV), repeated every 2–3 weeks for six cycles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulsed methylprednisolone IV (10 mg/kg) for 3 days, followed by oral steroid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide (15 mg/kg IV), repeated every 2–3 weeks for six cycles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with significant proteinuria (>500 mg daily) should receive ACE inhibitors or ARBs to prevent ESRD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Safety Considerations: Cyclophosphamide may cause hemorrhagic cystitis; minimise risk with hydration and mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate). Consider sperm or oocyte collection before treatment due to the risks of azoospermia and premature menopause."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide may cause hemorrhagic cystitis; minimise risk with hydration and mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consider sperm or oocyte collection before treatment due to the risks of azoospermia and premature menopause."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alternate Therapy: MMF can be used with high-dose glucocorticoids for renal involvement . It shows similar efficacy to cyclophosphamide with fewer side effects. Belimumab and Rituximab."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MMF can be used with high-dose glucocorticoids for renal involvement . It shows similar efficacy to cyclophosphamide with fewer side effects."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Belimumab and Rituximab."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulsed methylprednisolone IV (10 mg/kg) for 3 days, followed by oral steroid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide (15 mg/kg IV), repeated every 2–3 weeks for six cycles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide may cause hemorrhagic cystitis; minimise risk with hydration and mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consider sperm or oocyte collection before treatment due to the risks of azoospermia and premature menopause."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MMF can be used with high-dose glucocorticoids for renal involvement . It shows similar efficacy to cyclophosphamide with fewer side effects."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Belimumab and Rituximab."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gradual reduction of oral prednisolone from 40–60 to 10–15 mg/day within 3 months."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycophenolate and azathioprine are preferred; both are safer than cyclophosphamide."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aim for the lowest effective doses, regularly reassessing function and disease activity."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of hypertension presents with fever, rash, and joint pain. Laboratory tests show positive ANA and anti-histone antibodies. She has no signs of renal or neurological involvement. Which of the following medications is most likely responsible for her symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},{"t":"Hydralazine"},{"t":"Amlodipine"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hydralazine is a known trigger for drug-induced lupus, especially in patients with a history of hypertension. It is associated with positive anti-histone antibodies and symptoms consistent with lupus erythematosus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drug-Induced Lupus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is more common in whites and has less female predominance than SLE."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Linked to Antihistone antibodies and not associated with Anti-dsDNA Antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It rarely affects the kidneys or brain; symptoms usually resolve within several weeks after discontinuing the offending medication."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common offending agents for drug-induced lupus: (Options A,B and D ruled out) Antiarrhythmics: Procainamide, disopyramide, propafenone Antihypertensives: Hydralazine Antithyroid: Propylthiouracil Antipsychotics: Chlorpromazine, lithium Anticonvulsants: Carbamazepine, phenytoin Antibiotics: Isoniazid, minocycline, nitrofurantoin, dapsone Antirheumatics: Sulfasalazine Diuretics: Hydrochlorothiazide Antihyperlipidemic: Lovastatin, Simvastatin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antiarrhythmics: Procainamide, disopyramide, propafenone"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antihypertensives: Hydralazine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antithyroid: Propylthiouracil"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antipsychotics: Chlorpromazine, lithium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticonvulsants: Carbamazepine, phenytoin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotics: Isoniazid, minocycline, nitrofurantoin, dapsone"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antirheumatics: Sulfasalazine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics: Hydrochlorothiazide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antihyperlipidemic: Lovastatin, Simvastatin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antiarrhythmics: Procainamide, disopyramide, propafenone"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antihypertensives: Hydralazine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antithyroid: Propylthiouracil"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antipsychotics: Chlorpromazine, lithium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticonvulsants: Carbamazepine, phenytoin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotics: Isoniazid, minocycline, nitrofurantoin, dapsone"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antirheumatics: Sulfasalazine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics: Hydrochlorothiazide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antihyperlipidemic: Lovastatin, Simvastatin"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical considerations: In drug-induced lupus, ANA typically appears before clinical symptoms. It is important to test for ANA at the first indication of relevant symptoms and to consider discontinuing the suspected medication based on test results."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 45-year-old man with TB on ATT isoniazid therapy presents with fatigue, fever, joint pain, and a rash. Laboratory tests reveal a positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) test, and anti-histone antibodies are detected. Which of the following ANA staining patterns is most likely found in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Diffuse"},{"t":"Speckled"},{"t":"Nucleolar"},{"t":"Centromere"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation, along with positive anti-histone antibodies, suggests drug-induced lupus erythematosus associated with isoniazid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Homogeneous (Diffuse) pattern is the typical ANA pattern seen in Drug-induced lupus, which corresponds with anti-histone antibodies. ANA patterns of SLE"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ANA pattern Antibody identifies Clinical significance"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diffuse pattern Histones Significant for drug-induced lupus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Peripheral (rim) pattern Anti-ds-DNA Highly specific for SLE but not very"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sensitive"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Speckled pattern (Option Anti U1-RNP Associated with mixed connective B ruled out) tissue disease (MCTD)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti Sm Is specific for Lupus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti Ro (SS-A) Associated with Sjögren’s, SCLE &"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neonatal lupus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti La (SS-B) Associated with Anti-Ro"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti Scl-70 Associated with diffuse scleroderma Anti Jo-1 Associated with polymyositis with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pneumonitis and arthritis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nucleolar pattern RNA polymerase Associated with progressive (Option C ruled out) systemic sclerosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Centromere Kinetochore Associated with CREST, Sjogren’s (Option D ruled out) & thyroiditis"},{"k":"img","v":8730}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of recurrent miscarriages and recent episodes of deep vein thrombosis is found to have elevated anti-b2-glycoprotein I antibodies. Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is primarily responsible for her condition?","o":[{"t":"Deactivation of the complement pathway"},{"t":"Increased apoptosis of endothelial cells"},{"t":"Decreased platelet activation"},{"t":"Impaired fibrinolysis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario of a woman with recurrent miscarriages and deep vein thrombosis (DVT), along with elevated anti-b2-glycoprotein I antibodies, suggests antiphospholipid syndrome (APS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In APS, increased endothelial cell apoptosis exposes negatively charged phospholipids, promoting the formation of antiphospholipid antibodies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antiphospholipid Syndrome (APS) is an acquired thrombophilia mediated by autoantibodies, characterised by recurrent arterial or venous thrombosis and/or pregnancy-related complications. It predominantly affects females and can occur as:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary APS : Occurs independently."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary APS : Associated mainly with SLE and other autoimmune diseases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Target of Antibodies: The primary autoantibodies in APS target negatively charged phospholipids (PLs) and PL-binding proteins, such as b2-glycoprotein I (b2GPI) and prothrombin. Antibodies may also target:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lupus Anticoagulant"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiolipin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phosphatidylserine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Protein C"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is triggered by infections, oxidative stress, and significant physical stressors (surgery or trauma) in genetically predisposed individuals, resulting in increased apoptosis of endothelial cells and the exposure of negatively charged phospholipids (PL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exposed PLs bind to serum proteins such as b2-glycoprotein I (b2GPI) and prothrombin , forming neo-antigens . The immune system produces antibodies against these neo-antigens, leading to anti-PL antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The binding of anti-PL antibodies to disrupted endothelial cells initiates coagulation, resulting in thrombus formation."},{"k":"img","v":8733},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Manifestations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Venous Thrombosis: Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) (39%) Pulmonary Embolism Superficial Thrombophlebitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) (39%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superficial Thrombophlebitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arterial Thrombosis: Stroke (20%) Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) and Myocardial Ischemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stroke (20%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) and Myocardial Ischemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obstetric Complications: Recurrent first-trimester abortions (‡3). Unexplained death of a morphologically normal fetus after 10 weeks. Severe early pre-eclampsia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent first-trimester abortions (‡3)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unexplained death of a morphologically normal fetus after 10 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe early pre-eclampsia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hematologic findings: Thrombocytopenia and Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Livedo Reticularis : A mottled, lace-like purplish skin discolouration, often occurring with vascular lesions and aseptic bone necrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis : Characterized by small vegetations with organised platelet-fibrin microthrombi."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal involvement : May present as hypertension, elevated serum creatinine, and proteinuria, with associated thrombotic microangiopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) (39%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superficial Thrombophlebitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stroke (20%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) and Myocardial Ischemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent first-trimester abortions (‡3)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unexplained death of a morphologically normal fetus after 10 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe early pre-eclampsia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deactivation of the complement pathway (Option A): APS is primarily associated with antiphospholipid antibodies that activate the coagulation cascade rather than deactivate the complement pathway."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impaired fibrinolysis (Option C) : APS is mainly characterised by increased coagulation and platelet activation rather than a failure to break down clots."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased platelet activation (Option D) : In APS, the presence of antiphospholipid antibodies leads to increased platelet activation, contributing to thrombus formation."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a history of recurrent miscarriages, having experienced three miscarriages before 10 weeks of gestation and an unprovoked deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in her left leg six months ago, which required anticoagulation therapy. She is diagnosed with antiphospholipid syndrome. Which of the following is not a diagnostic criterion of antiphospholipid syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Vascular thrombosis of artery, vein or any small vessel"},{"t":"3 or more unexplained consecutive miscarriages before 10 weeks of gestation"},{"t":"Positive titer for APLA antibodies on a single occasion"},{"t":"One or more unexplained deaths of a normal fetus at or after 10 weeks"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory criteria for diagnosing Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA) include the presence of a positive titer for APLA antibodies on two occasions 12 weeks apart."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"APLA antibodies include"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lupus antibody"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticardiolipin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical criteria Vascular thrombosis - one or more clinical episodes of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"arterial, venous or small vessel thrombosis (Option A ruled out) Pregnancy morbidity (anyone) One or more unexplained deaths of a normal fetus at or after 10 weeks of gestation (Option D ruled out) One or more premature births of a normal neonate before 34 weeks of gestation due to eclampsia , severe pre-eclampsia or placental insufficiency Three or more unexplained consecutive miscarriages before 10 weeks of gestation (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lab criteria Intermediate or high titer of any one of the following"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"antibodies on two occasions 12 weeks apart. (Option C) Lupus antibody Anticardiolipin Anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of APLA: One clinical + one laboratory criterion in the absence of other thrombophilia"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 32-year-old woman with a known history of antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) was admitted to the hospital due to arterial thrombosis. She had a history of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) one year ago, for which she was treated with warfarin. She has been adherent to her warfarin therapy, with a target INR of 2.5. Investigations show the following reports. INR: 2.1 Lupus antibody: Positive Anticardiolipin antibody: Positive Anti-b2 glycoprotein I antibodies: Positive What would be the most appropriate adjustment to her treatment after this arterial thrombosis episode?","o":[{"t":"Administration of oral hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Increase the dose of warfarin with a target INR of 3.0 to 4.0"},{"t":"Switch to direct oral thrombin inhibitor"},{"t":"Switch to low-dose aspirin with prophylactic dose low molecular weight heparin."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Since this patient has experienced arterial thrombosis, the target INR should be raised from the standard range of 2.0-3.0 to 3.0-4.0. This adjustment can be achieved by increasing the dosage of warfarin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of antiphospholipid syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"After the first venous thrombosis Warfarin - Lifelong with a target INR 2.0-3.0"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Consider Direct thrombin inhibitors if there is a contraindication to warfarin or failure to achieve target INR *"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Arterial thrombosis Increase warfarin dose with target INR 3.0-4.0 or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option B) Maintain INR 2.0-3.0 and low-dose aspirin (LDA) 70-100 mg/day"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pregnant woman with APLA LDA + Prophylactic dose of LMWH (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"If thrombosis occurs - LDA + therapeutic dose of LMWH"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Recurrent obstetric complications, even after standard LDA + therapeutic dose of LMWH treatment Oral hydroxychloroquine 400 mg / day (Option A ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out) IV immunoglobulin 400 mg/kg for 5 days"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Asymptomatic patients with high-risk antiphospholipid Prophylactic low-dose aspirin profile Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome (CAPS) Combination therapy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Glucocorticoid Heparin Plasmapheresis or IV immunoglobulin"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Refractory CAPS B-cell depletion therapy with rituximab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Complement inhibition with Eculizumab"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"* Avoid direct thrombin inhibitors in cases of prior arterial thrombosis or triple positive APLA (Option C ruled out)"}]},{"n":14,"q":"Which of the following assay tests cannot detect lupus anticoagulant antibodies?","o":[{"t":"Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay"},{"t":"Activated thromboplastin time"},{"t":"Kaolin clotting time"},{"t":"Dilute Russell viper venom test"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay cannot detect lupus anticoagulants; instead, it detects anticardiolipin antibodies(gives false positive RPR for syphilis) and Anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I, which aids in the diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tests for detecting lupus anticoagulant:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Principle Interpretation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dilute Russell Utilises dilute Russell viper venom Presence of lupus anticoagulant Viper Venom to activate factor X in the clotting inhibits factor X activation , leading Time (DRVVT) cascade. to prolonged clotting time (no clot Test formation). (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Kaolin Clotting Measures the time taken for a clot Increased kaolin clotting time Time to form in the presence of kaolin, an indicates the presence of lupus (Option C ruled activator of the intrinsic pathway. anticoagulant, impairing normal out) coagulation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Activated Partial Assesses the intrinsic and common Prolonged aPTT suggests the Thromboplastin pathways of coagulation by presence of lupus anticoagulant, as Time (aPTT) measuring the clotting time after it binds to b2-glycoprotein I or (Option B ruled adding an activator to plasma. prothrombin and interferes with out) clotting."}]}]);
